{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Jacob vowed a vow, saying.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: what is the meaning of 'saying' (לאמר)? — to say to the generations, that they should vow in the time of their distress. And even though the Torah warned concerning vows and oaths, for the sake of a commandment (mitzvah) it is permitted. And so David said (Psalms 119): 'I have sworn and I will fulfill it,' etc." "<b>And give me bread to eat and a garment to wear.</b> This is the request of the righteous from HaShem: they do not ask for luxuries, but only the necessary thing alone, without which a person cannot live. <b>And know</b> that a person's inclination toward asking for luxuries brings him many troubles; therefore every person who fears HaShem should rejoice in his portion and be satisfied with little and not crave luxuries, and gladden his heart in the fear of HaShem — this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 15:16): 'Better is a little with the fear of YHWH than a great treasure and turmoil with it.' And he brought for this a sign and testimony and proof from the verse that follows it, which says (ibid.): 'Better a meal of vegetables where there is love than a fattened ox where there is hatred.' He says that it is better for people to have a little in company that is pleasing to them, more than abundance and delicacies in the company of haters." "<b>And this matter</b> he brought as a parable concerning the trait of contentment, and from it one may learn a fortiori (קל וחומר) that a person should reconcile himself and be satisfied with little in love and fear of the Blessed Name more than with increase of wealth gained through robbery and violence. And so Solomon the king, peace be upon him, prayed concerning the trait of contentment — this is what he said (Proverbs 30:8): 'Feed me with my allotted bread.' He explained that poverty and wealth are blameworthy traits that bring a person to transgress the commandments of the Torah, for with poverty a person will need to flatter people, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 18:23): 'A poor man speaks entreaties,' and with wealth a person comes to luxuries and acquires for himself arrogance and haughtiness of heart, in the manner of what is said (ibid.): 'And a rich man answers harshly,' until it makes him an abomination, as is said (Proverbs 16:5): 'An abomination to YHWH is every haughty heart.' And because of this he said: 'Poverty and wealth do not give me; feed me with my allotted bread.'" "<b>And know</b> that were it not that the imagination of man's heart is evil from his youth, lusting after the desire for excess wealth and honor in this world, it would not be fitting for him to worry about what he has not acquired of luxuries, but only to seek the necessary thing; for that which is necessary, the Holy One, blessed be He, will prepare for him day by day, in the manner of what is written in the section of the manna (Exodus 16:4): 'And they shall gather it day by day,' for it was not possible for them to store away manna for a set time, even from one day to the next, and since they trusted in the Blessed Name, He would prepare it for them day by day. And our Rabbis of blessed memory already expounded concerning the matter of livelihood: the Holy One, blessed be He, sustains the entire world from the horns of re'eimim to the eggs of lice, and this is what the verse says (Psalms 145:9): 'And His mercies are upon all His works.' And we already see with our eyes in the order of the world and its governance — which is with deep and wondrous wisdom — that the Holy One, blessed be He, prepares for His creatures and provides them the necessary thing, and what is more necessary is more available, and what is not so necessary is not so available. For example, pearls and kinds of precious stones, which are not so available in the world and are not in the possession of every person because they are not so necessary — for creatures can live without them. But food, which is more necessary than pearls, is more available than pearls, for you will find grain and food in markets and streets, whereas you will not find pearls. And water, which is more necessary than food — for hunger a person can endure two or three days, but not so thirst, for even a brief time a person cannot endure it — therefore water is more available than food, for you find them everywhere, in provinces and even on the roads. And air, which is more necessary than water — for the distress of thirst a person can endure a mil or a parshah, but without air a person, nor any living thing, can survive even a moment — therefore it is more available, so that you will not find any place empty of air, even in a place where a wall stands, the air is there. From this you learn that necessary things are more available in the world, for the Holy One, blessed be He, in His great wisdom, ordered His world with them and prepared them for His creatures. Therefore Jacob the righteous asked for bread to eat and a garment to wear, which is the necessary thing, and he asked for the trait of contentment — which is the request of the righteous — and did not ask for luxuries, for the Torah despises them, to the point that it withholds them even from the king, as is written (Deuteronomy 17:16–17): 'He shall not multiply horses for himself,' and 'he shall not multiply wives for himself,' and 'silver and gold he shall not greatly multiply for himself.' (Thus said the sage in the book Hovot ha-Levavot.)"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:13.097371632Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}